==English==

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:chaungen|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|changen}}, {{m|enm|chaungen}}, from {{der|en|fro|changier}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|cambiāre}}, from {{der|en|la|cambio|cambīre|to exchange, barter}}, derived from the noun {{m|la|cambium||change}} (whence was loaned the English {{lg|doublet}} {{dbt|en|cambium|notext=1}}), from {{der|en|cel-gau|cambion}}, earlier {{m|cel-gau||*kambyom|change}}, related to {{der|en|cel-pro|*kambos||twisted, crooked}}, possibly from {{der|en|ine-pro||*(s)ḱh₂(e)mbos}}, {{m|ine-pro||*(s)kh₂(e)mbos|crooked}}. More at {{l|en|skimp}}, {{l|en|scam}}; see also {{m+|ine-pro|*kh₂em-}}.

Cognate with {{cog|it|cambiare}}, {{cog|pt|cambiar}}, {{cog|ro|schimba}}, {{cog|scn|canciari}}, {{cog|es|cambiar}}. Used in English since the 13th century. {{displaced|enm|wenden}}, from {{m|ang|wendan||to turn, change}} (whence {{m|en|wend}}).

The noun is from {{inh|en|enm|change}}, {{m|enm|chaunge}}, from {{der|en|fro|change}}, from the verb {{m|fro|changier}}. See also {{m|en|exchange}}. Possibly related from the same source is Old English {{m|ang|gombe}}.

