===Verb===
{{wikidata lexeme|L33}}
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become something different.
#: {{ux|en|The tadpole '''changed''' into a frog.}}
#: {{ux|en|Stock prices are constantly '''changing'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|ergative}} To make something into something else.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|I|I|1|Lo I the man, whoſe Muſe whilome did maske,<br>As time her taught in lowly Shepheards weeds,<br>Am now enforſt a far unfitter taske,<br>For trumpets ſterne to '''chaunge''' mine oaten reeds,<br>And ſinge of Knights and Ladies gentle deeds{{…}}|stanza=1}}
#* {{quote-av|en|date=1988-3-21|title=w:Heart of Glory|series=w:Star Trek: The Next Generation|publisher=w:Paramount Domestic Television|oclc=26969098|text=KORRIS: They shunned you. Cursed you. Called you vile names, and you knew not why. Even now do you know why you are driven? Why you cannot relent—or repent—or confess—or abstain? How could you know? There have been no other Klingons to lead you to that knowledge.<br>WORF: Yes—yes—those feelings are still a part of me, but I control them. They do not rule me.<br>KORRIS: Yes, to fit in, the humans demand you '''change''' the one thing that you cannot '''change'''. But because you cannot—you do. That too is the mark of the warrior.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|title=The climate of Tibet: Pole-land|date=2013-05-11|volume=407|issue=8835|url=https://www.economist.com/science-and-technology/2013/05/11/pole-land|page=80|passage=Of all the transitions brought about on the Earth’s surface by temperature change, the melting of ice into water is the starkest. It is binary. And for the land beneath, the air above and the life around, it '''changes''' everything.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250531141113/https://www.economist.com/science-and-technology/2013/05/11/pole-land|archivedate=31 May 2025}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|2013|month=July–August|Catherine Clabby|Focus on Everything|w:American Scientist|http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/4/focus-on-everything||Not long ago, it was difficult to produce photographs of tiny creatures with every part in focus.{{…}}A photo processing technique called focus stacking has '''changed''' that. Developed as a tool to electronically combine the sharpest bits of multiple digital images, focus stacking is a boon to biologists seeking full focus on a micron scale.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170511100905/http://www.americanscientist.org:80/issues/pub/2013/4/focus-on-everything|archivedate=11 May 2017}}
#: {{ux|en|The fairy '''changed''' the frog into a prince.}}
#: {{ux|en|I had to '''change''' the wording of the ad so it would fit.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[replace]].
#: {{ux|en|Ask the janitor to come and '''change''' the lightbulb.}}
#: {{ux|en|After a brisk walk, I washed up and '''changed''' my shirt.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1971|Gwen White|Antique Toys And Their Background||193|Towards the end of the nineteenth century, boys who had pretended soldiers down through the centuries, now '''changed''' their pikes and swords for toy pistols.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[replace]] one's [[clothing]].
#: {{ux|en|You can't go into the dressing room while she’s '''changing'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The clowns '''changed''' into their costumes before the circus started.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[replace]] the [[clothing]] of (the one wearing it), especially to put a clean [[diaper]] on (someone).
#: {{ux|en|It’s your turn to '''change''' the baby.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To replace the sheets on (a bed) with fresh clean ones.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[transfer]] to another vehicle (train, bus, etc.)
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 December 2|author=Paul Bigland|title=My weirdest and wackiest Rover yet|journal=Rail|page=66|text=After stopping at these stations, my train has become busy. Returning day-trippers make up a goodly number, along with young people heading for a night out in Bristol, which is where I '''change''' once again.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[exchange]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|1|2|text=At the first sight / they have '''changed''' eyes. {{qualifier|exchanged looks}}}}
#* '''1662''' {{w|Sir Thomas Salusbury, 2nd Baronet|Thomas Salusbury}}, ''Galileo's Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems'' (Dialogue 2):
#*: I would give any thing to '''change''' a word or two with this person.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[change hand]] while riding (a horse).
#: {{co|en|to '''change''' a horse}}

