===Noun===
{{wikidata lexeme|L1259}}
{{en-noun|~}}
[[File:InternationalCoins.JPG|220px|thumbnail|right|[[loose change|Loose change]]]]

# {{senseid|en|noun}} {{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} The process or result of becoming [[different]].
#: {{ux|en|The product is undergoing a '''change''' in order to improve it.}}
#: {{ux|en|Such [[much-needed]] '''change'''s to the exam format might have a serious effect on candidates' performance.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2008|author=w:Nick Cave|album=w:Dig, Lazarus, Dig!!!|artist=Nick Cave and the Bad Seeds|title=Jesus Of The Moon|passage=Cause people often talk about being scared of '''change''' / But for me I'm more afraid of things staying the same}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|title=The climate of Tibet: Pole-land|date=2013-05-11|volume=407|issue=8835|page=80|url=http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21577341-worlds-third-largest-area-ice-about-undergo-systematic|passage=Of all the transitions brought about on the Earth’s surface by temperature '''change''', the melting of ice into water is the starkest. It is binary. And for the land beneath, the air above and the life around, it changes everything.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180217084317/https://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21577341-worlds-third-largest-area-ice-about-undergo-systematic|archivedate=17 February 2018}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Small [[denomination]]s of [[money]] given in exchange for a larger denomination.
#: {{ux|en|Can I get '''change''' for this $100 bill, please?}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[replacement]].
#: {{ux|en|a '''change''' of clothes}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2010-12-29||Mark Vesty|Wigan 2 - 2 Arsenal|w:BBC|http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/9322845.stm||After beating champions Chelsea 3-1 on Boxing Day, Arsenal boss Arsene Wenger made eight '''changes''' to his starting XI in an effort to freshen things up, with games against Birmingham and Manchester City to come in the next seven days.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221017180102/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/9322845.stm|archivedate=17 October 2022}}
## A [[replacement]] of a soiled [[diaper]] with a clean one; a diaper change.
##: {{ux|en|The baby needs a '''change'''}}
# {{senseid|en|balance of money}}{{lb|en|uncountable}} [[balance|Balance]] of money returned from the [[sum]] paid after [[deduct]]ing the [[price]] of a [[purchase]].
#: {{ux|en|A customer who pays with a 10-pound note for a £9 item receives one pound in '''change'''.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} An amount of [[cash]], usually in the form of [[coin|coins]], but sometimes inclusive of paper money.
#: {{ux|en|Do you have any '''change''' on you? I need to make a phone call.}}
#: {{ux|en|This bus ride requires [[exact '''change''']].}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A transfer between vehicles.
#: {{ux|en|The train journey from Bristol to Nottingham includes a '''change''' at Birmingham.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2019|month=October|author=John Glover|title=Heathrow rail expansion|journal=Modern Railways|page=72|text=It <small>[the Elizabeth Line]</small> will provide a 6tph (trains per hour) service and with a single '''change''' at Hayes & Harlington offer services towards Reading.}}
# {{lb|en|baseball}} A [[change-up]] [[pitch]].
# {{lb|en|campanology}} Any order in which a number of [[bell]]s are struck, other than that of the diatonic scale.
#* {{RQ:Holder Speech|passage=Four bells admit twenty-four '''changes''' in ringing.}}
# {{lb|en|Scotland|dated}} A [[public house]]; an [[alehouse]].
#* '''1727-1728''', {{w|Edward Burt}}, ''Letters from a Gentleman in the North of Scotland to his Friend in London''
#*: They call an alehouse a '''change'''.

